<section class="page-hero"><img class="bg" src="/assets/img/photos/icicles.webp" srcset="/assets/img/photos/icicles-640.webp 640w, /assets/img/photos/icicles.webp 1024w" sizes="100vw" width="1024" height="685" alt="" fetchpriority="high" decoding="async" data-parallax="0.12"><div class="wrap"><nav class="crumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb"><a href="/">Home</a><span>›</span><a href="/appraisal-solutions/">Services</a><span>›</span>Ice Dam &amp; Frozen Pipe Appraisal</nav><span class="kicker light up">Appraisal service</span><h1 class="up" style="animation-delay:.06s">Ice Dam and Frozen Pipe Appraisal in Wisconsin</h1><p class="lede up" style="animation-delay:.14s">Few places know winter water damage the way Wisconsin does. Ice dams creep water under shingles for weeks, and a single subzero night can split a pipe hidden in an outside wall. These claims look small at first and rarely stay that way.</p><div class="btn-row up" style="animation-delay:.22s"><a class="btn gold" href="tel:+18445371427"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M22 16.9v3a2 2 0 0 1-2.2 2 19.8 19.8 0 0 1-8.6-3.1 19.5 19.5 0 0 1-6-6A19.8 19.8 0 0 1 2.1 4.2 2 2 0 0 1 4.1 2h3a2 2 0 0 1 2 1.7c.1.9.4 1.8.7 2.7a2 2 0 0 1-.5 2.1L8 9.8a16 16 0 0 0 6 6l1.3-1.3a2 2 0 0 1 2.1-.4c.9.3 1.8.6 2.7.7a2 2 0 0 1 1.7 2z"/></svg> Call (844) 537-1427</a><a class="btn ghost-l" href="/contact-us/">Free claim review</a></div><div class="facts up" style="animation-delay:.3s"><div><small>Season</small><b>December to March</b></div><div><small>Hidden damage</small><b>Insulation, framing, mold</b></div><div><small>Key policy term</small><b>Heat maintained</b></div></div></div></section>
<div class="wrap layout"><article class="prose">
<p>Ice dam and frozen pipe appraisal in Wisconsin covers the winter claims that keep restoration crews busy every year. When the insurer accepts the loss but its estimate doesn’t reach far enough into the walls, ceilings and attic, we serve as your appraiser and build a scope that follows the water to where it actually went.</p>
<h2>How ice dams cause damage</h2>
<p>Heat escaping into the attic melts snow on the upper roof. The meltwater runs down to the colder eaves and refreezes, building a ridge of ice. More melt pools behind that ridge and works its way under the shingles, through the roof deck and into the house. The result is often stained ceilings, wet insulation, swollen window trim and peeling paint along exterior walls.</p>
<h3>What the estimate needs to include</h3>
<ul>
<li>Ice dam removal by steaming, which is safer for the roof than chipping</li>
<li>Roofing repairs at the eaves, including ice-and-water shield where code calls for it</li>
<li>Saturated attic insulation, which loses much of its value once wet and compressed</li>
<li>Ceiling and wall drywall or plaster, trim, and full-room painting for a uniform finish</li>
<li>Drying of wall cavities and framing, with moisture readings to confirm</li>
</ul>
<h2>Frozen and burst pipes</h2>
<p>Pipes in exterior walls, crawlspaces, garages and over unheated porches are the usual victims. The late January 2019 polar vortex brought widespread frozen pipe claims across the state, and every hard cold snap since has added more. A burst pipe often isn’t noticed until it thaws, by which time water has run for hours.</p>
<p>These losses tend to be larger than they first look. Water soaks downward through floors, follows framing and collects in basements. Hardwood floors cup, cabinets swell and finished lower levels need flood cuts. Mitigation records and moisture maps are central evidence.</p>
<h2>The policy fine print that matters</h2>
<p>Many policies exclude freezing damage unless you used reasonable care to maintain heat in the building, or shut off the water and drained the system. That condition comes up often with seasonal cottages and vacant homes. Whether you met it is a coverage question, not an appraisal question. Once coverage is accepted, appraisal can resolve how much the damage costs to repair.</p>
<div class="note"><b class="h">Seasonal property owners</b>If you close a cabin for the winter, keep records showing heat was set and checked, or that the water was shut off and lines drained. Those records can matter more than the estimate if the insurer questions coverage.</div>
<h2>Why winter claims get underpaid</h2>
<p>Ice dam leaks tend to appear in stages, so an adjuster may see only the first stain. Frozen pipe claims are often scoped before drying is complete, leaving damage that surfaces later. Both perils can also produce mold if moisture is left in place, and mold coverage is frequently limited. Getting the scope right early matters.</p>
<h2>Our approach</h2>
<p>We inspect the attic, roof edges and affected rooms, take moisture readings, review mitigation logs and price every room touched by water. If snow weight is also involved, our <a href="/appraisal-solutions/snow-load-roof-collapse-appraisal/">snow load appraisal</a> page explains how structural claims differ. For sudden water losses unrelated to cold, see <a href="/appraisal-solutions/water-sewer-backup-appraisal/">water and sewer backup appraisal</a>.</p>
<p>Cabin owners Up North and homeowners in places like <a href="/local-appraisers/wausau/">Wausau</a> or <a href="/local-appraisers/green-bay/">Green Bay</a> see these claims every winter. <a href="/contact-us/">Get in touch</a> if your insurer’s estimate stops at the stain.</p>

<h2>Common questions</h2><div class="faq"><details><summary>Are ice dams covered by homeowners insurance?</summary><div class="a"><p>Interior water damage from ice dams is commonly covered, while removing the ice itself and fixing the underlying ventilation or insulation may be treated differently. Check your policy wording.</p></div></details><details><summary>My cabin pipes froze while I was away. Will the claim be denied?</summary><div class="a"><p>Many policies cover it only if heat was maintained or the water was shut off and drained. If the insurer denies on that basis, it is a coverage issue to discuss with a Wisconsin attorney.</p></div></details><details><summary>Why is my ceiling still stained after the adjuster’s visit?</summary><div class="a"><p>Ice dam leaks often continue through a thaw cycle, so later damage may not appear in the first estimate. Report additional damage promptly and document it with dated photos.</p></div></details></div>
